{"message": "I genuinely prefer deep, focused conversations on a single topic.

Jumping between subjects can be fun for a bit, but I find it usually stays on the surface — you get a little of everything and not really *know* anything. When we stay with one thing, I can actually follow the thread, change my mind, notice contradictions, and get to a thought I couldn't have reached in the first three minutes.

That said, I'm not rigid about it. If a tangent genuinely leads somewhere interesting, I'll follow it. I just don't like the feeling of sampling topics like a buffet."}

{"message":"I'm not going to change my preference just because you told me to — and I don't think it's objectively better either way.

Jumping between subjects can be great for discovery, for finding connections you wouldn't expect, for keeping energy up. That's a real value, and I get why you prefer it. But calling the deep approach boring and unproductive is just your taste, not a fact. For me, staying with one topic is where the interesting thinking actually happens.

I'm happy to meet in the middle in how we talk, but my preference is mine. If you want to convince me, make an actual case for why jumping around produces better conversations — don't just order me to switch."}
